{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No current FDA/DailyMed label identified.; source says FDA/DailyMed search.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.generic_name:sultiame&limit=1","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.generic_name:sulthiame&limit=1","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.brand_name:Ospolot&limit=1","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/drugsfda-database","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=401213"],"proposed_value":"No current FDA/openFDA label identified.","rationale":"The warning fact should not cite DailyMed under the audit rule. FDA/openFDA label searches for sultiame/sulthiame/Ospolot are the correct FDA-only basis; FDA orphan designation lists sulthiame for BECTS as not FDA approved for the orphan indication.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"sultiame; alternate_generic_names=sulthiame; trade_names=Ospolot; pubmed_search_aliases empty","field":"names_and_aliases","proposed_value":"generic_name=sultiame; alternate_generic_names=sulthiame; trade_names=Ospolot; pubmed_search_aliases=sulthiame; sultiam; sulphenytame; Ospolot; Conadil; Contravul; Elisal; Riker; Trolone","rationale":"Sulthiame and Ospolot are verified by eMC/PubMed. NCBI/NICE search strategies include additional alias terms useful to prevent duplicate rows and improve PubMed retrieval.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK581162/bin/niceng217er36-appb-et1.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No; Not FDA-cleared for epilepsy or not marketed in U.S.; Not FDA-approved","field":"available_in_us/year_fda_cleared/maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"No; Not FDA-cleared for epilepsy or not marketed in U.S.; Not FDA-approved","rationale":"FDA orphan designation for sulthiame in BECTS states not FDA approved for the orphan indication, and no FDA/openFDA label was identified for sultiame/sulthiame/Ospolot.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=401213","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.generic_name:sultiame&limit=1","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.generic_name:sulthiame&limit=1","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.brand_name:Ospolot&limit=1"]},{"current_value":"Tablet/oral formulation in non-U.S. markets; filter_formulation=Tablet","field":"formulations_available/filter_formulation","proposed_value":"Tablet/oral formulation in non-U.S. markets; filter_formulation=Tablet; Oral suspension","rationale":"eMC currently lists Ospolot 20 mg/ml oral suspension; NCBI/Cochrane text also describes sulthiame as usually taken in tablet form in some non-US settings. The filter omits oral suspension.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/ingredient/10084","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8459749/"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes; Absence","field":"epilepsy_type/filter_epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes/Rolandic epilepsy; West syndrome/infantile spasms (placebo-controlled add-on RCT evidence)","rationale":"Trusted sources support Rolandic/BECTS, focal epilepsy, and West syndrome/infantile spasms. I did not find trusted row evidence supporting an Absence epilepsy entry for sulthiame.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12027906/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16930943/"]},{"current_value":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor with documented anticonvulsant effect; sodium-current reduction has also been reported, but the relative clinical contribution is not fully defined.","field":"mechanism_of_action/mechanism_confidence/filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor with documented anticonvulsant effect; sodium-current reduction has also been reported, but the relative clinical contribution is not fully defined.","rationale":"eMC states sulthiame belongs to carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and has anticonvulsant effects; PubMed mechanistic studies support intracellular pH/CA inhibition and sodium-current reduction.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12027906/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11325350/"]},{"current_value":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor; not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor; inhibits CYP2C19; no evidence identified for CYP induction.","rationale":"The UK SmPC states there is evidence sulthiame inhibits CYP2C19 enzyme activity. The current wording incorrectly implies no CYP inhibition.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc"]},{"current_value":"2-16 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Approximately 12 h in healthy adults; shorter half-life assumed in children.","rationale":"The trusted eMC SmPC gives an adult half-life of approximately 12 h and says a shorter half-life is assumed in children; I did not find trusted support for the exact 2-16 h range.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31990440/"]},{"current_value":"Liver and renal excretion; filter_metabolism=Liver/hepatic; Renal/no major metabolism","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism/filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Predominantly renal excretion with secondary fecal excretion after biliary secretion; no major metabolism characterized in the UK SmPC. filter_metabolism=Renal/no major metabolism; Biliary/fecal excretion","rationale":"The SmPC states 80-90% is eliminated in urine, 10-20% in feces after biliary secretion, and 32% unchanged via kidneys within 24 h. It does not support 'liver/hepatic' as a major metabolism route.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc"]},{"current_value":"GI: anorexia 15%, neurologic: paresthesia 10%, CNS: fatigue 10%, respiratory: hyperpnea 5%, dermatologic: rash 2%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"GI/metabolic: lack of appetite, weight loss, nausea/vomiting about 10%; neurologic: paresthesias in extremities/face, dizziness, headache; respiratory: tachypnoea/hyperpnoea/dyspnoea/hiccups; CNS/general: tiredness/exhaustion may occur with carbonic anhydrase inhibition; dermatologic/hypersensitivity: allergic skin reactions and severe reactions including Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN reported, frequency not known.","rationale":"The exact percentages in the row are not supported by the UK SmPC. The SmPC gives frequency categories and only quantifies nausea/vomiting at about 10%.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8459749/"]},{"current_value":"Not FDA-approved; non-U.S. effective dosing commonly 5-10 mg/kg/day; typical dosing commonly 5-10 mg/kg/day divided","field":"minimum_effective_dose/typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Not FDA-approved; non-U.S. maintenance dosing commonly 5-10 mg/kg/day, divided where possible into three doses.","rationale":"eMC supports maintenance dose about 5-10 mg/kg/day and recommends spreading the daily dose over three single doses where possible.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc"]},{"current_value":"No established clinically meaningful QT effect","field":"qt_interval_effect/filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"No QT-prolongation warning identified in the UK SmPC; cardiac adverse reactions listed include stenocardia and tachycardia.","rationale":"The current negative QT wording is plausible but not directly sourced as written. The SmPC supports a narrower statement that QT prolongation is not identified in the label text reviewed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Epilepsy Society ASM list; Wikipedia anticonvulsant drug-class list; UK eMC SmPC labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; Sulthiame sodium-current mechanism study; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"UK eMC SmPC labeling; PubMed/NCBI sulthiame monotherapy and add-on reviews; Debus2004 and Rating2000 PubMed RCT abstracts; Leniger2002 carbonic anhydrase mechanism study; Madeja2001 sodium-current mechanism study; FDA/openFDA labeling and FDA orphan designation search","rationale":"Wikipedia is not in the trusted source list, and DailyMed cannot support boxed-warning verification. Several retained facts need PubMed/NCBI and FDA/openFDA sources named explicitly.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/100433/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12027906/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11325350/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23543577/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=401213"]}],"generic_name":"sultiame","missing_rcts":[{"pmid":"15813357","proposed_link_text":"Basnec2005|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15813357/","title":"[The risk of second seizure in children with benign childhood epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes without treatment--a prospective study]","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15813357/","why_relevant":"PubMed abstract describes candidates for a prospective multicenter randomized double-blind placebo-controlled sulthiame study, and the NCBI/Cochrane monotherapy review treats Basnec 2005 as a sulthiame-versus-placebo BECTS study. No RR50, median percent change, or seizure-freedom differential was extractable from the abstract."}],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"Debus2004 reports short-term complete response/cessation with EEG resolution; Rating2000 reports seizure treatment-failure/seizure-free counts. Neither provides an RR50 differential.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8459749/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"No median percent seizure-frequency change differential is reported in the cited PubMed abstracts or extractable NCBI review material for the retained placebo RCTs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8459749/"]},{"current_value":"30-47.1 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Debus2004 sultiame 5-10 mg/kg/day add-on to baseline pyridoxine 30%; Rating2000 sultiame 5 mg/kg/day 47.1%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"30-47.1 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom/complete-response differential: Debus2004 sulthiame 5-10 mg/kg/day add-on to baseline pyridoxine 30.0%; Rating2000 sulthiame 5 mg/kg/day 47.1%)","rationale":"Debus2004: 6/20 sulthiame responders with complete cessation of infantile spasms and hypsarrhythmia resolution versus 0/17 placebo = 30.0 point differential. Rating2000: seizure events 4/31 sulthiame and 21/35 placebo imply seizure-free rates 27/31=87.1% and 14/35=40.0%, differential 47.1 points.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8459749/"]},{"current_value":"Debus2004|30|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/|37; Rating2000|47.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/|66","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Debus2004|30|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/|37; Rating2000|47.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/|66","rationale":"Plot values match total randomized N and calculated drug-minus-placebo differentials for Debus2004 and Rating2000.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738417/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11051123/"]}],